We are
The European Union (EU) is an economic and political partnership between 27 European countries. It plays an important role in international affairs through diplomacy, trade, development aid and working with global organizations. Abroad, the EU is represented through more than 140 diplomatic representations, known also as EU Delegations, which have a similar function to those of an embassy.
The EU Delegation to the United States of America, Washington D.C. works in close coordination with the Embassies and Consulates of the 27 EU Member States. We are a fully-fledged diplomatic mission and represent the European Union in dealings with the United States of America government in areas that are part of the EU’s remit.
We offer
The post of
Administrative Assistant-Procurement & Infrastructure Support (Local Agent Job Profile of Administrative Assistant, Group II)
in the Delegation’s Administration Section, under a fixed-term employment contract of two years, with a possibility of renewal under reserve of budgetary availabilities and operational considerations. For internal candidates holding an indefinite-term contract, alternative contractual arrangements may apply in accordance with institutional policies.
The team consists of 21 people and there are occasional atypical working hours. Within the Administration Section, the Infrastructure and Procurement team consists of 9 administrative professionals, all customer service focused, and working together to address the needs of colleagues in the Delegation. The team is responsible for building good relationships with external suppliers, procuring goods and services, implementing and managing contracts as well as managing the lifecycle of physical assets and capital projects within the Delegation’s building, all whilst bearing in mind sound financial management of public funds.
Under this post, the recruited person will be attributed functions depending on the needs of the Delegation, and on the changes of these needs. The successful candidate will serve under the supervision and responsibility of the Head of Administration providing procurement and infrastructure support.
The following main tasks and duties are currently required:
Preparation, coordination and support of public procurement procedures, linked to the administrative management of the EU Delegation and EU Residence in Washington, DC and for the San Francisco Office;
Administration and follow-up of infrastructure related contracts including, but not limited to, IT, cleaning, maintenance, repairs, transportation, security, insurance, fire extinguishers, pre-action, first aid etc., furniture and equipment for the Delegation, and contracts related to property management;
Responsible for planning and coordinating upcoming calls for tenders, ensuring that expiring contracts are renewed in a timely manner and that continuity of services is maintained. This includes monitoring contract expiry dates and consumption of awarded amounts, anticipating renewal needs, and initiating the tendering or renewal process sufficiently in advance;
Ensure financial and budgetary compliance of infrastructure matters with the Financial Regulations and other applicable legal and regulatory requirements;
Daily communication and coordination with vendors and service providers to regularly and properly execute contracted services and delivery of supplies in Washington, DC and San Francisco;
Administration and oversight of the Delegation’s subscriptions;
Asset Management/Inventory of Delegation furniture, equipment etc.;
Facility assistance - entering issues regarding plumbing, HVAC etc. into the Computer-aided Facilities Management (CAFM) System and liaising with the building management and colocators;
Assistance in various reporting (ex-post control, Annual Management Plan, financial reporting etc.);
Back-up for Head of Infrastructure Cell and for Procurement Officer;
Other ad-hoc administrative tasks related to the smooth running of the Delegation and the
Administration Section in general, including, but not limited to, back-up of other colleagues, escorting of contractors, assisting with representation events and VIP visits, and some light lifting if needed;
Operational Initiating Agent (OIA) for approving invoices;
Financial initiator for purchase orders.
In return, you will work in a section with a great team spirit and in an environment, which will allow you to develop professionally and deepen your understanding of the functioning of the European Union.
The base salary will depend on relevant and verified employment experience, typically starting from $95K (3 years’ experience), $97K (6 years’ experience), $103K (10 years’ experience) etc. There is a competitive benefits package including personal leave days and public holidays, flexible working hours, health insurance and 401k.
The expected start date will be 1 January or 1 February 2027.
Minimum requirements/eligibility criteria
(necessary for the application to be considered)
Minimum of High/Secondary school diploma or equivalent; a bachelor’s degree an advantage;
Minimum of 3 years of full-time paid work experience in procurement/contracting/project management. The professional experience must be acquired after the date on which the qualification allowing access to the post was obtained;
Excellent working knowledge of the English language;
Right to residence and work authorisation in the USA at the time of application. The European Union does not sponsor visas for Local Agents.
Selection criteria
University/Graduate degree or equivalent experience in procurement/contracting/project management related field an advantage;
Excellent communication and interpersonal skills; ability to work autonomously and as part of a team in synergy with the rest of the INFRA cell and Administration staff;
Excellent organizational skills, ability to multi-task and prioritize, and flexibility with regard to working hours;
Ability to work calmly under pressure and use own judgment and initiative; sense of responsibility, and motivation;
Must be professional, discreet, and able to safeguard confidential matters discussed by others during performance of duties;
Ability to deal with a varied range of people and working in a multicultural office, as part of an international team;
Excellent knowledge of IT tools (MS Office and web based apps; familiarity with online platforms (Webex, Zoom) would be an asset;
Excellent spoken and written command of the English language, knowledge of other European languages would be an advantage;
Experience of working in an EU or international organization, a diplomatic mission, or a multicultural environment would be an advantage;
Familiarity with the European Union and its Financial Regulation and IT systems (PPMT, SUMMA, ABAC Assets) would be an asset.
Excellent ability to communicate in a clear and structured manner, both verbally and in writing, and be customer service oriented.
How to apply
The applicant must apply by sending:
a signed cover/motivation letter;
indicate in your cover letter which type of US work authorisation you have (US citizenship, Green card, EAD);
a Curriculum Vitæ using one of the Europass templates: (
https://europass.europa.eu/en
). Other CV formats will be accepted.
The CV to include at the minimum, exact job titles, employers’ names and detailed tasks descriptions.
The resume must also include the names and contact details of at least two referees who can attest to your professional and personal attributes in the event you are shortlisted for the post. References will only be taken up for those who are shortlisted. For all traineeships, please indicate if these are paid or unpaid.
Please state in the subject of your email: Ref: 05/26 – INFRA – [YOUR SURNAME]. Send your completed application to
eeasjobs-048@eeas.europa.eu
by noon (EST) 18 November 2026. Applications will not be accepted from any other source.
Caution: The applicant must verify that he/she fulfils all the conditions relating to each document or application condition. The absence of one of these conditions or documents is a reason for the rejection of the application.
The process
After the deadline for applications, the eligible applications will be admitted to the Selection Committee set up for this purpose.
Depending on the number of applications received, successive phases of Selection may include shortlisting of candidates based assessment of the information provided in the cover letter, CV, practical testing and interviews. The best candidates will be invited for testing and interview.
Only candidates admitted to each successive selection phase will be contacted individually. The Delegation will not supply additional information or discuss the selection procedure. During the selection process, please do not contact the members of the Selection Committee, but address your questions and comments to the Delegation’s Administration at
delegation-usa-vacancies@eeas.europa.eu
.
The successful candidate may be subject to a background check.
Candidates who wish to lodge a complaint regarding the recruitment process may submit a written communication to the functional mailbox to which they sent the initial application. Upon receipt, the Delegation will review the matter and, where appropriate, consult Headquarters before providing a response.
